Question: https://leetcode.com/problems/count-number-of-bad-pairs/

Solution:
class Solution {
    public long countBadPairs(int[] nums) {
        long n =nums.length;
        long total_pairs = n*(n-1)/2;
        long count=0;
        Map<Integer,Long> fMap = new HashMap<>();
        for(int i=0;i<nums.length;i++) {
            int key = i-nums[i];
            if(fMap.containsKey(key)) {
                count+=fMap.get(key);
            }

            fMap.put(key,fMap.getOrDefault(key,0L)+1);
        } 

        return total_pairs - count;
    }
}

/*
    As we're repeatedly checking on numbers - hashing ! 
    // Thinking about hashKey 
    but here since we have two moving pointers - which makes simple hashing impossible, lets try mathematical invariants (Algebric Isolation)

    // conceptual derivation: bad_pairs = total_pairs - good_pairs

    total_pairs = n*(n-1)/2 
    good_pairs = Algebric isolation
    j-i != nums[j] - nums[i] -> turning to good pair
    j-i = nums[j] - nums[i]
    j - nums[j] = i - nums[i] -> so from this we can say
    k - nums[k] is the key for hashing
